@*
    A field whose values are plain strings and stay plain strings.

    `alternatives` is this shape, and so is every value of a key the schema does not
    define. A technology that was weighed and rejected was never written up, so
    there is nothing an address could name — it is never parsed as one and may not
    be dressed as one, whatever link resolver a host wires up. That is the
    distinction this component exists to hold: it takes no href and no navigation
    callback, so there is nothing here for a host to make followable.

    `aliases` used to be drawn through here and is not any more. The difference is
    that the term an alias names *is* modelled somewhere, so unlike a rejected
    technology it is a word a host can be asked to place — see MetadataAliasList,
    which is the same list with that one capability and a treatment to match.

    Each value is a MetadataScalar, so one string looks the same whether it
    arrived alone under `version` or as the third entry under `alternatives`. The
    values sit adjacent with no separator between them; the gap is the stylesheet's,
    so a value copied off the screen does not bring a comma with it.
*@

@foreach (var value in Values)
{
    <MetadataScalar Value="@value" ValueCssClass="@ValueCssClass" />
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The values, in the order the file wrote them. An empty list draws
    /// nothing at all — whether the row should be there is the caller's question,
    /// not this component's.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<string> Values { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The class each value wears, handed down to every entry so a list
    /// cannot end up dressed differently from a lone scalar.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? ValueCssClass { get; set; } = "knowledge-value";
}
